    Objects created from reflection classes provided by this module are wrappers over the actual C++ objects;
    for this reason comparing different objects with the @ref logical_equality_operator "==" or
    @ref logical_inequality_operator "!=" operators will not return the expected results.  To perform
    comparisons on reflection objects, use the \c isEqual() methods such as
    @ref Qore::Reflection::AbstractClass::isEqual() "AbstractClass::isEqual()",
    @ref Qore::Reflection::AbstractMember::isEqual() "AbstractMember::isEqual()", or
    @ref Qore::Reflection::Namespace::isEqual() "Namespace::isEqual()", among others.

    @section reflection_caller_privilege Reflection and the Privileges of the Calling Code

    Reflection follows the privileges of the code that uses it, not those of the reflection module:
    - calling a function or method, or constructing an object, through a reflection object is checked against the
      @ref sandboxing "sandboxing restrictions" of the calling code and of the @ref Qore::Program "Program" that the
      code belongs to, by the same rule as code written directly; a @ref Qore::Reflection::AbstractVariant "variant"
      obtained from a reflection object is checked the same way
    - reading or writing a member, constant or global variable through a reflection object is checked against the
      functional domain of its class in the same way
    - navigating the namespaces, classes, functions, constants or values of another @ref Qore::Program "Program"
      through a reflection object, or reading or writing its values, is allowed only:
      - from that @ref Qore::Program "Program";
      - from a @ref Qore::Program "Program" that created it, directly or through @ref Qore::Program "Program"
        objects that it created, including one created as an independent @ref Qore::Program "Program" (with the
        \c independent argument of the @ref Qore::Program::constructor() "Program constructor"), which inherits
        nothing from the code that creates it but is controlled by it; or
      - from code at least as privileged as it: code whose restrictions forbid no functional domain that the target
        allows, that allows every functional domain that the target must allow explicitly (for example the debugger
        domain with @ref Qore::PO_ALLOW_DEBUGGER "PO_ALLOW_DEBUGGER"), and that no sandbox other than the target's own
        governs; the code of a user module is not less privileged for lacking a domain that a
        @ref Qore::Program "Program" must allow explicitly, as such options belong to the programs that use the
        module, so a program with @ref allow-injection "%allow-injection" or @ref allow-debugger "%allow-debugger"
        can hand its types to modules such as @ref dataproviderintro "DataProvider"

      otherwise \c REFLECTION-ACCESS-ERROR is raised
    - a class, typed hash or enum declared in one @ref Qore::Program "Program" and inherited by or imported into
      another one keeps no path into the declaring @ref Qore::Program "Program": its namespace, as returned by
      reflection, is the namespace with the same path in the @ref Qore::Program "Program" of the reflection object

